A||||12||jlhuber||formula vs gt||||5||
Z||000000||jlhuber||09-25-2000||04:05 PM||||what are the mechanical differences between the '88 gt & formula?  the people i talk to say i should go with a formula if i can find one but i like the nose of the gt better.  the noses should be interchangeable for the most part, right?  has anyone ever stuck a gt nose on a formula?  any pics?  the reason is that i am working on a road rocket that is going to use a syclone style motor (4.3L turbo).  i'm usig a 4.3 instead of a 5.0 or 5.7 because of size issuses.  the 4.3 should give me more room for the turbo.  besides 300-400hp should be plenty for my fiero.  for the intercooler set up i am using a custom system that i saw where the a/c system is used as a liquid-to-air i/c.  when you want "mo-go" turn on the a/c and you've got frigid intake air.  the drawback is that the a/c uses hp to work.  hopefully the boost will counter that as long as i launch it like a syclone.  anyway, i am looking to settle a dispute between me and my ...uh, collegues, about what the differences in the two models are.  thanks for the help.   -jess||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||09-25-2000||05:09 PM||||Mechanically the two are exactly the same. The GT and the Formula have the same WS6 suspension and the same HO 2.8-liter engine.<P>The only difference is that the GT weighs 50 lbs. more than the Formula.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||09-25-2000||07:05 PM||||The GT's generally had more options on them.  In fact, a couple of the options like leather seats were not available on the GT.  For pure speed, a Formula with very few options would be the lightest and therefore slightly faster.  <P>With the small differences, I'd choose whichever has the best chasis for your engine project, whether its a GT or Formula.  Sounds like a cool project  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">  Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/Motorcity/Boulevard/9278/fierosig1.jpg">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||09-25-2000||07:30 PM||||Watch what an ECM scanner shows you about the AC compressor at high engine loads/speeds. To prevent damage and to allow for more acceleration power, it is disengaged.||||reg||1
Z||000004||gamman||09-25-2000||07:52 PM||||Hey guys, further to this discussion, how different is an 88 4 cyl in comparison to the 88 v6's?<P>I mean, could I just put the ws6 suspension on? <P>Or is there more differences (things that can't be "bolted" on?-frame stuff)?<P>Think about why I am asking, usually a 4 cyl will sell for $1000 cheaper (or more) so you are money ahead, if you can just bolt on the differences.<P>Also, does the 4cly have a getrag? I doubt it, but I haven't found info anywhere about the 88 4cyl.<P>Thanks,<BR>Ger.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DjKid||09-25-2000||09:03 PM||||Am I wrong in saying that the tranny from a 88 v6 will bolt up to the 88 4banger?  It should since the isuzu will bolt to the v6..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||09-25-2000||10:08 PM||||The Coupe has the same suspension and brakes as the 4mula and GeeTee, however, the Coupe did not have the rear swaybar. I *think* the springs are the same, but haven't had a set of each to compare. I *think* the rear springs are the same, but the V6 cars were longer (Ed Park's told me that). You can add a 1" rear bar to the Coupe(phormula's and GeTe's came whith 7/8") like I did. There are mounting holes on the Coupe's cradle for the bar, but you need the brackets to connect the bar to the struts. The front bars are the same - 1" on all 88's.<P>The GT and Formula were different... the Formula could not be ordered with power mirrors or the Performance Sound (subwoofer) and was only available with the black wheels.<P>The Coupe came with the Isuzu 5 speed and the V6 cars came with the Getrag 5 speed. The Isuzu will bolt to the V6 (I have one in my 88 V6 Coupe as well as the 1" rear bar).<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR><B>KEEP THE EXCITEMENT ALIVE!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||09-25-2000||10:30 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SCCA FIERO:<BR><B>... the Formula could not be ordered with power mirrors or the Performance Sound (subwoofer) and was only available with the black wheels.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's what I thought, too.<BR>However...<BR>According to Mark Corbin's Fiero Spotters Guide, there were 94 Formulas built with power mirrors and a whopping 8 built with the subwoofer sound system. Not sure where the info came from though.<P>Think we'll ever see one? I'm not holding my breath.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'll try to find the spring codes on my stickers. Think I remember seeing them, once. Will let you know what I find.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||gixxer||09-26-2000||12:18 AM||||Found a spring guide awhile back. For the '88s, the numbering went like this:<BR>Fronts:<BR>FPJ  STIFFEST<BR>FPH     |<BR>ARD     ||<BR>ARB  SOFTEST<BR>Rears:<BR>PUD  STIFFEST<BR>PUC     |<BR>PUB     |<BR>PUA  SOFTEST<BR>To correlate these numbers to the RPO list on yer LF fender, delete the first letter off the codes and add:<BR>"6" for left front,<BR>"7" for right front,<BR>"8" for left rear,<BR>"9" for right rear.<BR>So the numbers on my A/C equipped Formula look like this:<BR>6RD (left front, medium)<BR>7RD (right front, medium)<BR>8UC (left rear, semi-stiff)<BR>9UC (right rear, semi-stiff)<BR>Works for me! How about you guys...<BR>  ||63.15.2.136||reg||1
Z||000009||1FieroGTRacingInc||09-26-2000||01:00 AM||||gixxer...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Thank's for the great info. I was thinking just this afternoon if there was a way to read some of those codes. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||gamman||09-26-2000||03:28 AM||||Sorry to add to this excellent post in a wierd way, but if you want to start an new post go ahead.<P>I am looking at fiero projects in Canada, and there are very few 88 v6's for affordable money. People think they have a collector car (no offense - the cheapest I have seen is $4000 -90M needs clutch and engine smokes! or $5600 for a nice one 50M.) BUT there are lots of 4 cyl 88's for around $3000(or less) with good mileage(60+M).<P>SO, for $1000, would I be able to turn the suspension into the v6 style?<P>I plan on modifying engine anyway, so the smoking one is actually looking better....too bad it has such high mileage.<P>Ger.<p>[This message has been edited by gamman (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||09-26-2000||07:17 PM||||Since the suspension is probably worn out anyway, you should be able to get a car with the base suspension and start replacing parts.  For around a grand, you should have a suspension that will far outperform the stock WS6 suspension.  Start with the small stuff (poly bushings, larger sway bar, shocks, struts) and work your way up to the more expensive ones (like springs and new, lower profile tires).  The new wheels and tires will run you over a thousand bucks, but the rest should be less.||||reg||1
Z||000012||gamman||09-26-2000||09:31 PM||||Any sites that you recommend for this stuff?<P>I will drive the car on the street, so I still want to keep my fillings. Also, I live where it snows (a lot - in fact, it has already), so I need a "non-harsh" suspension.<P>Is this asking too much? Can I have my cake and eat it too? Or should I just stick with a middle rated spring and WS6 package?<P>G||||reg||1
